<!--
    A function whose flow runs on into another function's body is not made
    `async` by that body's calls to the task runtime.

    Issue: a function the metadata does not bind is `async` when it calls
    the task runtime's entry points (`swift_task_alloc`, `swift_task_switch`,
    ...). The calls were taken from the whole recovered flow, so a plain
    function whose flow reached into an async neighbour (a jump table read
    past its end, a branch into a shared tail) got that neighbour's
    `async_context` as its first parameter. Seen on an arm64 iOS app: an
    enum-to-string switch whose unchecked byte-indexed table ran on into
    unrelated code printed as `sub_102cafa90(swift.type_metadata
    *async_context,...)`.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 prototype models (`kuna_swiftcall.rs`
    `callee_evidence`): only the calls between the function's entry and
    the next known entry count.

    Assertions: `kuna_pick` (0x2c90) branches past the prologue of
    `kuna_alloc` (0x2ca0), so its flow holds that function's call to
    `swift_task_alloc` and the store through r14 after it; it takes the
    selector alone. `kuna_alloc` itself calls `swift_task_alloc` and
    keeps its context.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
